\subsection{Gene Regulation Network based Block}
\label{Perturbation}
To improve the understanding of single-cell perturbations, we propose a novel GRN block that models gene interactions and incorporates perturbation-specific information. Starting from the GRN adjacency matrix $A$ (Eq.~\ref{GRN_construct}), we assign each gene a learnable embedding, resulting in a gene embedding matrix $G = [g_1, g_2, \dots, g_N]^T \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times D}$, where $g_i$ denotes the embedding of gene $i$ and $D$ is the embedding dimension. We then construct a condition-specific embedding matrix $G_{\mathbb{P}}=[g_{\mathbb{P}}^{1},g_{\mathbb{P}}^{2},\dots,g_{\mathbb{P}}^{N}]^{T}$, where $\mathbb{P} \in \{\texttt{gene}, \texttt{mole}, \texttt{ctrl}\}$ corresponds to gene perturbations, molecular perturbations, and the unperturbed group, respectively.

Specifically, when it comes to $\mathbb{P}=\tt{ctrl}$, we fuse the initial gene embeddings $G$ with the timestep $t$, the cell state $c$, and the noisy input $x_t$. This process can be formally expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\tt{ctrl}}^{i}=\Phi(g_{i},t,c) +  \Psi_{x_{t}}(x_{t,i}) \in R^{D}
\end{equation}
where $\Phi$ and $\Psi_{x_t}$ are both Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) that project the input into the same embedding space. 

Similarly, when $\mathbb{P}=\tt{mole}$, the perturbation condition $P=\{\mathbb{S},\mathbb{D}\}$, where $\mathbb{S}\in R^{D_{\mathbb{S}}}$ denotes the representation of the drug molecule extracted by the pre-trained molecular model \cite{Uni_Mol}, and $\mathbb{D}\in R$ represents the drug dose. These representations are then fused together through an MLP, $\Psi$, to obtain a combined perturbation condition embedding $F_{\mathbb{S,D}}=\Psi(\mathbb{S},\mathbb{D})\in R^{D}$.

Given that different genes exhibit distinct sensitivities and associations with drugs and their doses, simply merging the representations may fail to capture the true regulatory relationships. Therefore, we propose a method to further integrate the molecular and gene representations, allowing the model to effectively learn the complex relationships between genes and molecular perturbations:
\begin{equation}
    F_{\tt{mole}}^{i}=\Phi_{f}(\Phi(g_{i},t,c)\|F_{\mathbb{S,D}})
    \label{eq:mole_gene_specific}
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_{f}$ is an MLP that fuses the output of $\Phi(g_{i},t,c)$ and perturbation embedding $F_{\mathbb{S,D}}$. Finally, we obtain the embedding as:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\tt{mole}}^{i}= F_{\tt{mole}}^{i}+\Psi_{ctrl}(ctrl_{noisy,c,i}) +  \Psi_{x_{t}}(x_{t,i}) \in R^{D}
\end{equation}
where $\Psi_{ctrl}$ encodes noisy unperturbed group information specific to cell type $c$ and gene $i$.

In the case of $\mathbb{P}=\tt{gene}$, the perturbation condition is given by $P={k}$, which biologically corresponds to the knockout of a specific gene $k$. We incorporate this perturbation information as follow:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\tt{gene}}^{i}=  \Phi(g_{i}\odot \bar{M}_{i},t,c) +  \Psi_{x_{t}}(x_{t,i}) \in R^{D}
\end{equation}
where $\odot$ denotes Hadamard Product, $\bar{M}_i \in \mathbb{R}^D$ is a mask vector defined for the gene $i$. When $i = k$, $\bar{M}_i$ is a zero vector; otherwise, it is a vector of ones.

After completing the above steps, we perform message passing based on the GRN $A$ to aggregate information across genes. The resulting representation is given by:
\begin{equation}
    F^{l+1}=\frac{1}{H} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \operatorname{GAT}^{h}(A,F^{l})
    \label{eq:GRNnetwork}
\end{equation}
where $H$ represents the number of head, and $F^{0}$ is initialized as the embedding matrix $G_{\mathbb{P}}$, as defined earlier. The $\operatorname{GAT}$ used for feature aggregation can be found in \cite{GAT,GAT2}. The final output of the GAT layers is $\widetilde{G}_{\mathbb{P}}=[\widetilde{g}_{\mathbb{P}}^{1},\widetilde{g}_{\mathbb{P}}^{2},\dots,\widetilde{g}_{\mathbb{P}}^{N}]$. Finally, we obtain the embedding $F_{GW}\in R^{N}$, which contains gene-wise information (Eq.~\ref{gene_wise}). This embedding summarizes the perturbation effects for each individual gene and is passed to other model modules for downstream processing.
\begin{equation}
    F_{GW,i}=W_{i}\odot \widetilde{g}_{\mathbb{P}}^{i}+b_{i} \in R
    \label{gene_wise}
\end{equation}
where $W_{i},b_{i}$ denote specific parameters corresponding to gene $i$.



