\begin{abstract}
The ring lattice with $n$ nodes, each connected to the $2k$ nearest neighbors, has been utilized as the basis for several models of small-world networks, obtained by rewiring or adding edges to generate shortcuts. It is well-known that when a rumor process is embedded in these networks, the system exhibits a transition between localization and propagation at a finite value of the network mean degree, emphasizing the importance of incorporating shortcuts to optimize the dissemination process. We adopt a complementary approach by examining the rumor process exclusively within the context of the ring lattice. Our objective is to determine the optimal value of $k$ as a function of $n$ to maximize the dissemination of the rumor. We assume that the lattice is subdivided into three classes of agents: ignorants, spreaders, and stiflers. A spreader transmits the rumor to any of its ignorant neighbors at rate one. At the same rate, a spreader transitions to a stifler after contact with other spreaders or stiflers. The rumor model is a continuous-time Markov chain, which can be used to model the state of each node at any given time. Following an analysis of contrasting examples, and an examination of the general case through a numerical approach, we demonstrate that, even in the absence of shortcuts, the model can exhibit a behavior concerning the proportion of nodes reached by the information that is analogous to that observed in homogeneously mixed populations. This is observed provided $k=k(n)$  is of the order of $\log n$.  
\end{abstract}

