\section{Conclusion}

We consider the Maki-Thompson rumor model on a $2k$-regular ring lattice with $n$ nodes. After discussing theoretical results for two extreme cases—the complete graph ($k = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$) and the cycle graph ($k = 1$)—we carry out a numerical analysis using Monte Carlo simulations for general values of $k$. Our focus is on how the behavior of the process depends on $k$ as a function of $n$. We find that the final proportion of ignorant individuals is approximately the same as in the complete graph, provided that $k(n)$ is on the order of $\log n$. The significance of studying the ring lattice lies in the fact that this type of graph forms the basis for several small-world network models, such as the Watts–Strogatz network and its Newman–Watts variant. For these networks, it is well known that when a rumor spreads, the process undergoes a transition between localized and propagation at a specific level of network randomness. These findings highlight the importance of adding shortcuts to improve information dissemination in such networks. In our work, we demonstrate that even without shortcuts, the model can exhibit behavior—regarding the proportion of informed nodes—similar to that observed in homogeneously mixed populations. Since the Maki-Thompson model is one of the earliest mathematical models proposed for rumor spreading and has inspired extensive research in information transmission on graphs, our analysis can be adapted to address more general models of social contagion.






