\section{Conclusion}
We provide a comprehensive analysis of neural network approximation capabilities for spectral Barron functions, significantly lowering the smoothness requirements compared to existing theories. By establishing approximation rates for both shallow and deep networks, we show that functions with small smoothness can achieve substantial approximation efficiency. Our findings indicate that shallow networks attain Monte Carlo rates for functions in $\mathscr{B}^{1/2}$, while deeper networks further reduce the necessary smoothness requirements. The sharpness of the approximation rates is confirmed, with lower bounds matching the upper bounds, up to a logarithmic factor. Future research could explore higher-order convergence results for spaces with greater smoothness.
