\noindent
{\it Spatio-temporal simulations.}
To put these ideas on solid grounds, we have performed ST simulations 
by considering the example of the NEE model, because it provides the minimal ingredients for a non-trivial space-time coupling, whereby different frequency components (colours) diffract along different cone angles.
%-- high temporal frequencies diffract less than low frequencies.
The NEE is obtained from the UPE (\ref{eq:upe}) with the approximations: 
%for the linear and nonlinear dispersion relations: 
${\tilde \beta}_m({\tilde \omega})=\beta_m (1+{\tilde \omega}/\omega_o)^{-1} - \sum_{j \ge 2} \kappa_j {\tilde \omega}^2$, where $\kappa_j=\frac{1}{j!}\partial_\omega^j k(\omega_o)$ denote high-order dispersion effects, and 
$\Gamma_m({\tilde \omega})=   1+{\tilde \omega}/\omega_o$, with ${\tilde \omega}=\omega-\omega_o$ the frequency offset (see Appendix~I).
At variance with the experiments carried out so far to study light thermalization \cite{PRL20,pourbeyram22,mangini22,zitelli24,podivilov22,PRL22,PRL23,ferraro23,ferraro24,ferraro25,kirsch25}, here the injected beam is spatio-temporally incoherent: the different $(\omega,m)$-components $b_m(\omega,z=0)$ are independent complex-valued Gaussian random variables of zero mean; each spatial mode $m$ has a Gaussian spectrum as a function of $\omega$ with the same FWHM and different amplitudes, see dark blue curves in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(a,d-f).



