\section{\label{sec:Intro}Introduction}
The electron, a cornerstone of modern physics and technology, remains an enigmatic entity. Conventionally modeled as a point-like particle carrying charge $ e=1.602\times 10^{-19} ~\text C $, its electromagnetic behavior is fully described by the Lorentz-covariant four-current $j=(c q, \pmb{j})$, where $ q=q(\pmb{r},t) $ and $\pmb{j}=\pmb{j}(\pmb{r},t)$ denote the charge and current densities~\cite{JDJackson1999}. This four-current governs both the generation of, and interaction with, the electromagnetic field $ A=(\frac{\phi}{c},\pmb{A}) $, where $ \phi $ and $ \pmb{A} $ are the scaler and vector potentials, respectively:
\begin{equation}
\square A=\mu _ {0} j;  \quad
\mathcal{L}_{\text{int}} = - j A. 
\end{equation}\label{GenerationAndInteraction}
where $ \square $ is the d'Alembertian operator and $ \mathcal{L} $ is the interaction Lagrangian. 

However, electrons also exhibit wave-like behavior, as demonstrated by double-slit interference~\cite{jonsson1961elektroneninterferenzen,jonsson1961electron,tonomura1989demonstration} and electron diffraction phenomena~\cite{davisson1927diffraction,thomson1927diffraction}. This necessitates a quantum mechanical description, wherein the four-current is expressed in terms of the wavefunction $ \Psi(\pmb{r},t)$:
\begin{equation}\label{fourcurrent}
q(\pmb{r},t)=e\Psi^{\dagger}(\pmb{r},t)\Psi(\pmb{r},t); \quad
\pmb{j}(\pmb{r},t)=ec\Psi^{\dagger}(\pmb{r},t)\pmb{\alpha}\Psi(\pmb{r},t). 
\end{equation}

The wavefunction evolves according to the Dirac equation~\cite{Dirac28,dirac1930principles}:
\begin{equation} \label{Dirac}
i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi (\pmb{r},t)=\left[ -i\hbar c \pmb{\alpha} \cdot \pmb{\nabla }+\gamma ^0 m_{e}c^2\right]\Psi(\pmb{r},t),
\end{equation}
where $ m_e $ is the electron mass, $ c $ is the speed of light, and $ \hbar $ is the reduced Planck constant. $\pmb{\alpha}$ and $ \gamma ^0 $ are Dirac-matrices. As a relativistic wave equation, the Dirac formalism allows the quantum four-current to encompass both wave dynamics and spin characteristics in a Lorentz-covariant manner. 

For an energy eigenstate satisfying $i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \Psi=\mathcal{E}\Psi$, we can derive from the Dirac equation:
\begin{equation}\label{current} 
\pmb{j}(\pmb{r})=\frac{ec^2}{\mathcal{E}}\left\{\pmb{\nabla}\times\left(\Psi^{\dagger}(\pmb{r})\frac{\hbar}{2}\pmb{\Sigma}\Psi(\pmb{r})\right)+i\frac{\hbar}{2}\left[\left(\pmb{\nabla}\Psi^{\dagger}(\pmb{r})\right)\Psi(\pmb{r})-\Psi^{\dagger}(\pmb{r})\left(\pmb{\nabla}\Psi(\pmb{r})\right)\right]\right\},
\end{equation} 
where $\pmb{\Sigma}=\frac{1}{2 i}\pmb{\alpha} \times \pmb{\alpha} $ is the spin operator. The first term represents a circulating spin-associated component, while the second corresponds to translational motion via the momentum operator $-i\hbar\pmb{\nabla}$. This expression shows that the electron wave inherently carries spin, even in the absence of an external magnetic field. 

The observed spin value $ \frac{\hbar}{2} $ arises from interactions with external fields~\citep{Ohanian86,GaoJOPCO22}, confirming that spin is a spatially structured, intrinsic property of the wave---termed wave-spin---rather than an abstract attribute of a point-like particle. Clearly, the wave here represents more than just a probability distribution.  

We therefore propose that the electron wave be treated as the sole physical entity, inherently carrying both charge and spin in a Lorentz-covariant manner. This stands in contrast to the conventional quantum mechanical interpretation~\cite{bohr1928quantum}, characterized by wave-particle duality, in which the wave is viewed as a probabilistic abstraction governing the behavior of a point-like particle.

In our framework, the particle with a defined size and shape does not exist. Apparent particle-like behaviors, such as those observed in cathode ray tubes or electron beam lithography, are instead attributed to the small collision cross-section of the electron wave when interacting with other physical objects, analogous to that of electromagnetic waves. 

Importantly, the wave-entity is proposed as a real, physical object, distinct from the wavefunction, which is a mathematical vector in Hilbert space that calculates observable quantities and evolves deterministically according to the Dirac equation. In this interpretation, the apparent collapse of the wavefunction reflects a natural physical transition of the wave-entity from one state to another, rather than the disappearance of a real object. This view departs from the probabilistic framework of quantum mechanics: well-defined quantum objects behave deterministically, and computational machines based on quantum processes produce definite outcomes.

Identifying the fundamental nature of a physical entity is essential for accurately interpreting physical phenomena. By treating the wave as the sole physical entity, we propose a framework that may help resolve paradoxes inherent in the particle-based and probabilistic interpretations of quantum mechanics. In this view, the electron does not transition instantaneously or randomly between discrete locations---a notion incompatible with special relativity and causality---but instead exists as a spatially extended wave that is shaped by boundaries. In Einstein–Podolsky–Rosen (EPR)-type entanglement, the electron’s spin is not a localized property of a point particle but a spatially distributed wave characteristic that can diffuse rapidly through free space~\citep{GaoJOPCO22}. This extended nature of the wave-entity prevents any distant observer from accessing the full spin information, yet enables broader connectivity than a localized particle, eliminating the need for nonlocal or superluminal communication. A full treatment of entanglement within this wave-based framework will be presented in future work.

In this paper, we focus on the superposition of spin states forming a spin qubit, which is a foundational element in quantum computing~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}. Much like a Schrödinger’s cat~\cite{schrodinger1935gegenwartige}, the qubit invites conceptual paradox: how can an electron simultaneously be both spin-up and spin-down? We resolve this by explicitly deriving the four-current for an electron confined in a three-dimensional cavity. Our results show that the qubit corresponds to a well-defined current density configuration, thereby removing the paradox. Furthermore, the relative phase between qubit states that is essential for quantum computation~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}manifests as a real-space orientation of the current density, providing a concrete physical interpretation. 

Finally, we investigate the magnetic fields generated by the qubit via $\square A = \mu_0 j$, leading to testable predictions that distinguish the wave-entity interpretation from the traditional wave-particle paradigm. This theoretical and exploratory work is intended to stimulate scientific discussion, invite peer feedback, and encourage experimental efforts that could help clarify foundational entities, resolve conceptual paradoxes, and ultimately advance our understanding of quantum phenomena.

\section{\label{sec:ElectronCavity}Electron Wave-Spin in a Cavity}
In this section, we derive the analytical wavefunctions and current densities of an electron confined in a finite cylindrical quantum dot, modeled as a three-dimensional cavity with partial radial confinement and complete axial confinement. Our analysis reveals that the confined Dirac electron exhibits a toroidal wave-spin topology, which fundamentally differs from the conventional particle-based interpretation of spin. This provides a concrete demonstration of spin as an extended spatial structure rather than an intrinsic point-like attribute.

We begin with the Dirac equation in the presence of a cylindrically symmetric potential:
\begin{equation} \label{Diracwithpotential}
i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi (\pmb{r},t)=\left[ -i\hbar c \pmb{\alpha} \cdot \pmb{\nabla }+\gamma ^0 m_{e} c^2+U(\pmb{r})\right]\Psi(\pmb{r},t),
\end{equation}
where $ U(\pmb{r}) $ models the quantum dot confinement~\cite{loss1998quantum,hanson2007spins}:
\begin{equation}\label{potential}
U(\pmb{r})=\Biggl\{
\begin{array}{ccc}
0, & 0<\rho<R;-d<z<d & \text{Region I}\\
U, & \rho>R;-d<z<d & \text{Region II}\\
\infty,& z<-d; z>d & \text{Region III} \\
\end{array}
\end{equation}

The Dirac momentum operator in cylindrical coordinates $(\rho,\phi,z)$ is expressed as:
\begin{equation}\label{momentum}
\pmb{\alpha} \cdot \pmb{\nabla}
=\alpha _{\rho }\frac{\partial }{\partial \rho }+\alpha _{\phi }\frac{1}{\rho }
\frac{\partial }{\partial \phi }+\alpha _z
\frac{\partial }{\partial z},
\end{equation}
with the cylindrical-coordinate commutation and anticommutation relations:
\begin{equation}\label{alpha}
\alpha_{\rho }^2=\alpha_{\phi }^2=\alpha_{z}^2=1,[\alpha _{\rho },\alpha_{\phi }]=2i \Sigma_z, \{ \alpha_{\rho },\alpha_{\phi }\}=0. 
\end{equation}

The electron is fully confined along the $ z $-axis but only partially confined radially, giving rise to an evanescent wave outside the cavity previously discussed~\cite{EntropyEvaSpin2024}. While the Schrödinger solution for this geometry has been discussed for planar systems~\cite{lobanova2004cylindrical}, we solve here the full Dirac equation to expose the relativistic wave-spin structure.

We assume a stationary solution of the form:
\begin{equation}
\Psi (\pmb{r},t)=e^{-i\mathcal{E}t/\hbar}
\psi (\rho,\phi,z),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{E}$ is the eigenenergy. We solve for the wavefunction $ \psi (\rho,\phi,z) $ in Regions I and II, with the wavefunction vanishing in Region III due to the infinite potential. As established in~\cite{EntropyEvaSpin2024}, the simplified treatment inside the cavity remains valid, despite the presence of evanescent components near the boundary. 

By separation of variables, we obtain eigenfunctions for spin-up $\psi_{nlm\uparrow}(\rho,\phi,z)$ and spin-down $\psi_{nlm\downarrow}(\rho,\phi,z)$ states labeled by quantum numbers:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}\label{psinlmup}
\psi_{nlm\uparrow}(\rho,\phi,z)=\Biggg\{
\begin{array}{cc} N \left(
\begin{array}{c}
J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
 0 \\
-i \eta_{\text{I}} k_m J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\sin(k_m z) \\
\eta_{\text{I}} e^{i \phi} \{i \frac{1}{2}\zeta_{nlm}\left[ J_{l-1}(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )-J_{l+1}(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )\right]-i \frac{l}{\rho } J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )\}e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{I}\\\\
\kappa_{nlm} N \left(
\begin{array}{c}
K_l(\xi_{nlm} \rho)e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
 0 \\
-i \eta_{\text{II}} k_m K_l(\xi_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\sin(k_m z) \\
\eta_{\text{II}} e^{i \phi} \{i \frac{1}{2}\xi_{nlm}\left[-K_{l-1}(\xi_{nlm} \rho)-K_{l+1}(\xi_{nlm} \rho)\right]-i \frac{l}{\rho } K_l(\xi_{nlm} \rho)\}e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z)  \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{II}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext};
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}\label{psinlmdown}
\psi_{nlm\downarrow}(\rho,\phi,z)=\Biggg\{
\begin{array}{cc} N \left(
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\
J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
\eta_{\text{I}} e^{-i \phi} \{i \frac{1}{2}\zeta_{nlm}\left[ J_{l-1}(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )-J_{l+1}(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )\right]+i \frac{l}{\rho } J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )\}e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
i \eta_{\text{I}} k_m J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\sin(k_m z) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{I}\\\\
\kappa_{nlm} N \left(
\begin{array}{c}
 0 \\
 K_l(\xi_{nlm} \rho)e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z) \\
\eta_{\text{II}} e^{-i \phi} \{i \frac{1}{2}\xi_{nlm}\left[-K_{l-1}(\xi_{nlm} \rho)-K_{l+1}(\xi_{nlm} \rho)\right]+i \frac{l}{\rho } K_l(\xi_{nlm} \rho)\}e^{il\phi}\cos(k_m z)  \\
i \eta_{\text{II}} k_m K_l(\xi_{nlm}  \rho )e^{il\phi}\sin(k_m z) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{II}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}
where $e^{il\phi}$ represents the azimuthal wavefunction with quantum number $l=0,1,2...$. $ \cos(k_m z) $ represents the $z$-axis standing wave, where $ k_m=\frac{m \pi}{2d} $ for quantum number $ m=1,3,5... $. $J_{l}(\zeta_{nlm}  \rho )$ and $ K_l(\xi_{nlm} \rho) $ are the radial wavefunctions in terms of Bessel functions of the first and second kinds, where $\zeta_{nlm}$ and $\xi_{nlm}$ are the radial wave vectors defined in  relationship to the eigenenergy $\mathcal{E}_{nlm}$ by:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{zetaxi}
\zeta_{nlm}^2&=&\frac{\mathcal{E}_{nlm}^2-m_{e}^2 c^4-\hbar ^2 c^2 k_m^2}{\hbar ^2 c^2}; \nonumber \\
\xi_{nlm}^2&=&\frac{(\mathcal{E}_{nlm}-U)^2-m_{e}^2 c^4-\hbar ^2 c^2 k_m^2}{\hbar ^2 c^2 }. \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}

The eigenenergy $\mathcal{E}_{nlm}$ and matching coefficient $ \kappa_{nlm}$ are determined via boundary conditions at $ \rho=R $: 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{boundarynlm}
J_l(\zeta_{nlm} R ) &=&\kappa_{nlm} K_l(\xi_{nlm} R);\nonumber \\
\eta_{\text{I}} \{i \frac{1}{2}\zeta_{nlm}\left[ J_{l-1}(\zeta_{nlm}  R )-J_{l+1}(\zeta_{nlm}  R )\right]-i \frac{l}{R } J_l(\zeta_{nlm}  R )\}&=& \kappa_{nlm} \eta_{\text{II}}  \{i \frac{1}{2}\xi_{nlm}\left[-K_{l-1}(\xi_{nlm} R)-K_{l+1}(\xi_{nlm} R)\right]-i \frac{l}{R } K_l(\xi_{nlm} R)\}, \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}
where the quantum number $n=1,2,3...$ is implicitly contained in the boundary conditions. 

The geometric factors $\eta_{\text{I}}$ and $\eta_{\text{II}}$ are: 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eta}
\eta _{\text{I}}&=&\frac{\hbar c}{\mathcal{E}_{nlm}+m_{e} c^2};\nonumber\\
\eta _{\text{II}}&=&\frac{\hbar c}{\mathcal{E}_{nlm}-U+m_{e} c^2},  
\end{eqnarray}
which are also quantum state specific, converging in the non-relativistic limit to:
\begin{equation}\label{eta2}
\eta _{\text{I}}\approx \eta _{\text{II}}\approx\eta=  \frac{\hbar}{2m_{e}c}.
\end{equation}

For the ground state ($n=1$, $l=0$, $m=1$), we simplify notation by writing $ k_1=k=\frac{\pi}{2d} $, $\zeta_{101}=\zeta$, $ \xi_{101}=\xi $, and $\kappa_{101}=\kappa  $. The spin-up and spin-down wavefunctions become:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}\label{psi101up}
\psi_{\uparrow}=\psi_{101\uparrow}(\rho,\phi,z)=\Biggg\{
\begin{array}{cc}N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
J_0(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
 0 \\
-i \eta k J_0(\zeta \rho )\sin(k z) \\
-i \eta e^{i \phi} \zeta J_1(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{I}\\\\
\kappa N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
K_0(\xi \rho)\cos(kz) \\
 0 \\
-i \eta k K_0(\xi \rho )\sin(kz) \\
-i \eta e^{i \phi} \xi K_1(\xi \rho)\cos(kz)  \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{II}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext};

\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}\label{psi101down}
\psi_{\downarrow}=\psi_{101\downarrow}(\rho,\phi,z)=\Biggg\{
\begin{array}{cc}N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
 0 \\
J_0(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
-i \eta e^{-i \phi} \zeta J_1(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
i \eta k J_0(\zeta \rho )\sin(k z) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{I}\\\\
\kappa N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
0 \\
K_0(\xi \rho)\cos(kz) \\
-i \eta e^{-i \phi} \xi K_1(\xi \rho)\cos(kz)  \\
i \eta k K_0(\xi \rho )\sin(kz) \\
\end{array}
\right),  & \text{II}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}

These states are degenerate and can be superposed without energy splitting, enabling qubit constructions without oscillatory interference.

The eigenenergy $\mathcal{E}$ and matching coefficient $ \kappa$ satisfy the boundary conditions:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{boundary101}
J_0(\zeta R)&=&\kappa K_0(\xi R);\nonumber \\
\zeta J_1(\zeta R)&=& \kappa \xi K_1(\xi R), 
\end{eqnarray}
and the wavefunction is normalized such that the total charge equals that of a single electron: 
\begin{equation}\label{N2}
N^2=\frac{1}{2\pi d \left[\int_0^R J_0 (\zeta_{n01}  \rho )^2 \rho d\rho +\kappa ^2 \int_R^{\infty }K_0 (\xi_{n01}  \rho )^2 \rho d\rho \right]}.  
\end{equation}

Finally, we derive the explicit form of the current density in the spin-up ground state: 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{current101}
j_\phi(\rho,z)&=&\Biggl\{
\begin{array}{cc}
-2 N^2 ec \eta \zeta_{n01} J_0(\zeta_{n01} \rho) J_1(\zeta_{n01} \rho)\cos^2 (k z), \text{Region I}\\\\
-2 N^2 \kappa^2 ec \eta \xi_{n01} K_0(\xi_{n01} \rho) K_1(\xi_{n01} \rho)\cos^2 (k z), \text{Region II}\\ 
\end{array} \nonumber \\
j_\rho(\rho,z)&=&0, \, \text{everywhere} \nonumber \\
j_z(\rho,z)&=&0, \, \text{everywhere}
\end{eqnarray}

The azimuthal current density underscores the wave-spin nature of the electron, revealing a toroidal topology that is absent in the particle-spin model. The current density extends beyond the cavity confinement, confirming the presence of an evanescent wave-spin. 

For a cavity characterized by $R=8 \text{nm}$, $d=4 \text{nm}$ and  $U=10 \text{meV}$, we begin by calculating the eigenenergies and corresponding wave vectors using Eq.~\ref{boundary101}. These serve as the base for determining all wave-spin parameters via Eqs.~\ref{zetaxi},\ref{eta}, and \ref{N2}. This procedure enables us to compute and visualize the current density topology in a three-dimensional contour plot, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig1}. For contrast, the conventional spinning-ball model of the electron is also depicted, highlighting the differences between the two models.

These findings reinforce the view that the electron wave is a spatially extended physical entity that cannot be reduced to a mathematical point without disrupting continuity and losing information, as seen in discussions such as those involving black holes~\cite{UnruhWald2017}. Its topological structure encodes intrinsic information that is persistent and irreducible, resisting loss through mathematical abstraction or simplifying assumptions.

The resilience of these topological features to environmental disturbances opens new possibilities for more stable and fault-tolerant qubit designs in quantum information technologies. The fundamental toroidal structure of the ground state, along with the emergence of multi-toroidal configurations in excited states, lays the groundwork for a new class of quantum computing architectures rooted in the tangible, topological nature of the electron wave-spin.

