\section{\label{sec:CavitySpinCylinder}Electron wave-spin qubit}
The electron spin qubit is represented as a superposition of the spin-up and spin-down states, expressed by Eq.~\ref{psi101up} and \ref{psi101down}:
\begin{equation} \label{Qubit}
\psi=\cos \frac{\Theta}{2} \psi_{\uparrow} +\sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi}\psi_{\downarrow},
\end{equation}
where $0\leq \Theta \leq \pi$ and $0 \leq \Phi \leq 2\pi$ uniquely define the qubit's state. The parameter $\Theta$ controls the amplitude distribution between the spin-up and spin-down components, satisfying the normalization condition $\cos^2 \frac{\Theta}{2}+\sin^2 \frac{\Theta}{2}=1$. The relative phase $ \Phi $ governs the quantum interference between the two states. Together, $\Theta$ and $\Phi$ determine the qubit's position on the Bloch sphere\cite{bloch1946nuclear}, with logical operations represented as rotations on this sphere~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}.

The relative phase $\Phi$ plays a central role in quantum computing. In the Quantum Fourier Transform (QFT)\cite{cleve1998quantum,coppersmith2002approximate}, relative phases accumulate and encode periodicity in the amplitudes, enabling efficient solutions to problems like factoring in Shor’s algorithm\cite{shor1999polynomial}. In Grover’s search algorithm~\cite{grover1997quantum}, constructive and destructive interference driven by phase inversion amplifies the correct solution. More generally, quantum phase manipulation is central to algorithms in quantum information science~\cite{nielsen2002quantum} such as Deutsch–Jozsa~\cite{deutsch1992rapid}. In our framework, this significance is made manifest through the current density and its corresponding magnetic field, both of which directly link to the relative phase $\Phi$ of the spin qubit.

The full wavefunction of the spin qubit follows directly from combining Eqs.~\ref{psi101up},\ref{psi101down}, and Eq.~\ref{Qubit}:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}\label{psiQubit}
\psi=\Biggg\{
\begin{array}{cc}N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
 \cos \frac{\Theta}{2} J_0(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz)\\
\sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi}J_0(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
-i \cos \frac{\Theta}{2}\eta k J_0(\zeta \rho )\sin(k z) -i\sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi} \eta e^{-i \phi} \zeta J_1(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz) \\
 -i\cos \frac{\Theta}{2} \eta e^{i \phi} \zeta J_1(\zeta \rho )\cos(kz)+i \sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi} \eta k J_0(\zeta \rho )\sin(k z) \\
\end{array}
\right)  & \text{Region I}\\\\
\kappa N\left(
\begin{array}{c}
\cos \frac{\Theta}{2} K_0(\xi \rho)\cos(kz) \\
\sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi}K_0(\xi \rho)\cos(kz)\\
 -i\cos \frac{\Theta}{2} \eta k K_0(\xi \rho )\sin(kz) -i \sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi}\eta e^{-i \phi} \xi K_1(\xi \rho)\cos(kz) \\
-i \cos \frac{\Theta}{2} \eta e^{i \phi} \xi K_1(\xi \rho)\cos(kz)+i \sin \frac{\Theta }{2} e^{i\Phi}\eta k K_0(\xi \rho )\sin(kz) \\
\end{array}
\right)  & \text{Region 1I}
\end{array},
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}
from which we can compute the current densities in each coordinate:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{currentQubit}
&&j_\rho=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
-N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \sin(\Phi-\phi) k J_0(\zeta \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{I}\\
-\kappa^2 N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \sin(\Phi-\phi) k K_0(\xi \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{II}
\end{array}; \nonumber \\
&&j_\phi=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
-2 N^2 e c \eta\cos\Theta  \zeta J_0(\zeta \rho) J_1(\zeta \rho)\cos^2(kz)+N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \cos(\Phi-\phi) k J_0(\zeta \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{I}\\
-\kappa^2 2 N^2 e c \eta \cos\Theta  \xi K_0(\xi \rho) K_1(\xi \rho)\cos^2(kz)+\kappa^2 N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \cos(\Phi-\phi) k K_0(\xi \rho)^2\sin(2kz),  & \text{II}
\end{array}; \nonumber \\
&&j_z=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
2 N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \sin(\Phi-\phi) \zeta J_0(\zeta \rho)J_1(\zeta \rho)\cos^2(kz), & \text{I}\\
\kappa^2 2 N^2 e c \eta \sin\Theta \sin(\Phi-\phi) \xi K_0(\xi \rho)K_1(\xi \rho)\cos^2(kz), & \text{II}
\end{array}. \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}
\end{widetext}

In the special case where $\Theta=\frac{\pi}{2} $, corresponding to an equal superposition of spin-up and spin-down states, the current density simplifies to:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{currentQubit2}
&&j_\rho=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
-N^2 e c \eta \sin(\Phi-\phi) k J_0(\zeta \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{I}\\
-\kappa^2 N^2 e c \eta \sin(\Phi-\phi) k K_0(\xi \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{II}
\end{array}; \nonumber \\
&&j_\phi=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
N^2 e c \eta \cos(\Phi-\phi) k J_0(\zeta \rho)^2\sin(2kz), & \text{I}\\
\kappa^2 N^2 e c \eta \cos(\Phi-\phi) k K_0(\xi \rho)^2\sin(2kz),  & \text{II}
\end{array}; \nonumber \\
&&j_z=\Big\{
\begin{array}{cc}
2 N^2 e c \eta \sin(\Phi-\phi) \zeta J_0(\zeta \rho)J_1(\zeta \rho)\cos^2(kz), & \text{I}\\
\kappa^2 2 N^2 e c \eta \sin(\Phi-\phi) \xi K_0(\xi \rho)K_1(\xi \rho)\cos^2(kz), & \text{II}
\end{array}. \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}
\end{widetext}

Notably, the current density depends on the difference between the relative phase $ \Phi $ and the azimuthal angle. This means that the relative phase, an abstract parameter on the Bloch sphere, manifests physically in real space through the observable current densities. Specifically, the components of the current density, $ j_\rho $ and $ j_\phi $, are proportional to the vector $ (\sin(\Phi-\phi),\cos(\Phi-\phi) ) $ in cylindrical coordinate. As a result, the current density in the $ x-y $ plane points in the direction perpendicular to $ \phi=\Phi $. Thus, the relative phase $ \Phi $ directly determines the orientation of the current density, and together with the parameter $ \Theta $, determines the overall configuration of the current density. 

This real-space manifestation of the qubit is visualized through a three-dimensional contour plot of the current density, shown in Fig.\ref{fig:fig2}(b). The current circulates around an axis aligned with the direction $\phi = \Phi$, making the physical influence of the relative phase explicit. From the wave-entity perspective, the superposition of the spin states does not produce a "Schrödinger's cat"-like paradox, as illustrated in Fig.\ref{fig:fig2}(a), but instead results in a coherent and well-defined configuration of current density. 

The wave-spin model further reveals that the qubit extends beyond the cavity as an evanescent wave, indicating that quantum information is not entirely confined within the physical boundary. In the following section, we show that the magnetic field generated by the qubit also penetrates into the surrounding space. This spatial extension of both the electron wave and its associated fields highlights the inherent connectivity among quantum systems. Crucially, this interdependence arises naturally from the wave dynamics, without requiring "nonlocal" phenomena to explain quantum correlations or information transfer.

