A direct consequence of the existence of a wetting film is that, at equilibrium, the pressure deficiency at the air/liquid interface at height $z$ in a gravity field is fixed by hydrostatic. Consequently, after a transient during which the disorder of the pressure field will generates flows, the pendular skeleton is expected to present reach an equilibrium state presenting a curvature gradient, and thus a gradient in the volume of the bridges. In our description of the funicular state, we neglect the volume variation due to the effect and consider that the elements of water added to form clusters can be described by an identical volume $v$. The hydrostatic pressure gradient plays a role on the domain of existence of the funicular state: as clusters must be bounded by interfaces of curvature $\kappa_c \pm \Delta \kappa_c$, it can exist at equilibrium only in a band of width $2\Delta z_c = 2l_c^2 \Delta \kappa_c$ (see fig.~\ref{fig:model}).


