\section{Introduction}

Let $d$ be a natural number and let $\Omega\subset \mathbb R^d$ be a domain. Throughout this paper we denote by $\mathcal H(\Omega)$  the space of harmonic functions in $\Omega$. Recall that a function is called harmonic in a certain domain if its Laplacian is equal to zero everywhere in this domain.
 We call a subset $S\subset \Omega$ a uniqueness set for the space $\mathcal H(\Omega)$ if for a function $f\in \mathcal H(\Omega)$ we have
$$f\restr S=0, f\in \mathcal H(\Omega) \text{ \;\; implies \;\; } f(x)=0 \text{ \;\; for all } x\in \Omega.$$
In this paper we shall only concentrate ourselves on the uniqueness sets for the space $\mathcal H(\mathbb R^d)$.

\begin{rem}
	All harmonic functions considered in this paper are harmonic in the whole space, unless we specify the contrary.
\end{rem}

Of course, if a certain harmonic function vanishes on an open set or on a closed rectifiable curve then it is trivially zero, simply by the maximum principle. Some examples of nontrivial sets of uniqueness for $\mathcal H(\mathbb R^d)$ are given in~\cite{kokurin2015sets}. These sets are  nonanalytic curves. A general description of uniqueness sets for the space $\mathcal H(\mathbb R^d)$ seems to be out of reach even if one restricts the considerations  to those sets that are finite unions of affine subspaces of $\mathbb R^d$.

Notice that in the plane the corresponding question can be answered rather easily. Indeed, let $E=\{p_j\}_{j=1}^N$ be a finite set with $p_j\in \mathbb C$. Then the polynomial defined for $z\in \mathbb C$ by the following formula
$$P_E(z):=\prod_{j=1}^N(z-p_j)$$
is holomorphic, nonzero and vanishes at the set $E$. This means that the polynomial $\Re(P_E)$ is hence the desired harmonic function.

Recall the connected Problem 151 from the Scottish Book, posed by Rolin Wavre, see~\cite{mauldin2015scottish}.

\bigskip

\begin{quote}
`November 6, 1936. Prize: A ``fondue'' in Geneva. Original manuscript in French.

\bigskip

Does there exist a harmonic function defined in a region which contains a cube
in its interior, which vanishes on all the edges of the cube ? One does not consider
$f=0$.'
\label{quote:one}
\end{quote}
\bigskip



In this paper we construct such a function. In more detail we have the following theorem.


\begin{theorem}
	\label{labell}
For any natural number $d\geqslant 3$, there exists a nonzero harmonic function  on the whole space $\mathbb R^d$, which vanishes on the $d-2$ skeleton of the unit cube $Q_d$.
	\end{theorem}
	We denote here for $0\leqslant k \leqslant d$ by $S_{k,d}$ the $k$-skeleton of the unit cube $Q_d:=[-1/2,1/2]^d$.
By the $k$-skeleton we mean the union of $k$-dimensional faces
of the unit cube, that is to say the set of all points that belong to the boundary of the cube and have at least $d-k$ coordinates equal either to $-1/2$ or to $1/2$.



	\begin{rem}
	Note that Theorem~\ref{labell} is just false in the case where $d=2$, simply by the maximum principle for harmonic functions.
\end{rem}

	We remark that the structure of the nodal sets of harmonic functions is a very interesting classical topic that remains popular until nowadays. Probably, the first questions in this domain date at least to the second part of XIX-th century from the classical work of Maxwell see~\cite{maxwell1873treatise}. This work is related to the Maxwell conjecture which still remains open, see~\cite{zolotov2023upper} for recent important results on this conjecture.  For other recent advances in the study of the nodal sets of harmonic functions we cite for example papers~\cite{logunov2015ratios},~\cite{logunov2016ratios},~\cite{greilhuber2024quadratic},~\cite{gichev2009some},~\cite{de2010geometric} and~\cite{de2020weighted} to name a few.
	%Let us describe some already known results in connection with our main subject~\cite{agranovsky2000problem}.

\medskip


We are already in position to show how one may construct the desired harmonic function in first theorem. Consider the  function defined for  $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_d)\in \mathbb R^d$ by the following  formula
$$
f_d(x_1,\ldots,x_d):=\prod_{1\leqslant i<j\leqslant d}(x_i^2-x_j^2),
$$
see Figure~\ref{fig:3zero} for the picture of the nodal set of the function $f_3$. Notice that the functions $f_d$ resemble the Vandermonde polynomials, see e.g.~\cite{macdonald1998symmetric}.
\begin{rem}
	Notice that the function $f$ defined just above is not the unique nontrivial function that enjoys the conditions of in Theorem~\ref{labell}. Another function that works is defined for $x\in \mathbb R^d$ by
	$$
g(x):=x_1\cdot\ldots \cdot x_d \cdot \prod_{1\leqslant i<j\leqslant d}(x_i^2-x_j^2).
$$
\end{rem}

It is an interesting question to describe all harmonic functions that vanish at the edges of the unit cube.

\begin{rem}
	Notice also that the function $h$ defined for $x\in \mathbb R^d$ by
	$$h(x):=e^{x_1}\cdot \ldots \cdot e^{x_d} \prod_{1\leqslant i<j\leqslant d}(x_i-x_j)$$ 
	satisfies 
	$$
\Delta h(x)= d h(x),
$$
which means that the function  $h$ is a Laplace eigenfunction.
\end{rem}

Here is the main technical auxiliary result of the current paper.
\begin{lemma}
\label{lem1}
Let $f_d$ be as above and let $d\geqslant 2$ be a natural number. We have
$$\Delta f_d(x)=0,$$
for all $x\in \mathbb R^d$, where $\Delta=\sum^d_{k=1} \partial^2/\partial x_k^2$ denotes the Laplacian operator.
\end{lemma}

Indeed, the proof of Theorem~\ref{labell} boils down to that of Lemma~\ref{lem1}, since for each element $e$ of the skeleton $S_{d-2,d}$ of the unit cube of dimension $d\geqslant 3$ and  each point $x\in e$ one has either $x_k=x_l=\pm 1/2$ or $x_k=-x_l=\pm 1/2$ for a certain pair of coordinates $x_k, x_l$ with $(k,l)\in [1,\ldots, d]^2$. This  means that $x_k^2-x_l^2=0$ and in turn that  $f\restr e=0$ and we are done.

We shall give two different proofs of our Lemma~\ref{lem1}. The first proof will be rather direct and computational. In the second proof we shall make use of the fact that the classical Vandermonde polynomial is harmonic and, moreover, that it annihilates certain  general second order differential operator, see~\cite{konig2001eigenvalues}.


\bigskip

Another goal of this paper is to construct  certain new families of harmonic polynomials that vanish at the same set in the unit ball in $\mathbb R^n$ for all $n\geq 4$. To do this, we use the theory of harmonic morphsims. 
\begin{theorem}
	\label{labell2}
In each space $\mathbb R^{2k}, k\geq 1$ there exist  irreducible nondegenerate quadratic homogenous harmonic polynomials  that divides infinitely many linearly independent harmonic polynomials.

In each space $\mathbb R^{2k+1}, k\geq 3$ there exist irreducible nondegenerate  homogenous harmonic polynomials that divide infinitely many linearly independent harmonic polynomials.
%
%In $\mathbb R^{5}$, there exists an irreducible nondegenerate  harmonic polynomial $\mathcal P$ that divides infinitely many linearly independent harmonic polynomials.
\end{theorem}
It is maybe worth noting that by nondegenerate polynomial in $\mathbb R^n$ we mean here a polynomial that nontrivially depends on the all $n$ variables of  $\mathbb R^{n}$ . Notice that our Theorem~\ref{labell2} extends results by Logunov and Malinnikova from~\cite{logunov2015ratios}, Section 4. Indeed, in~\cite{logunov2015ratios} the authors constructed such a family in dimension four using a different idea that axially-symmetric harmonic functions in  $\mathbb R^4$ can be reduced to ordinary harmonic functions of two variables, see~\cite{khavinson1991reflection}.

\bigskip

Let us now mention an interesting question with connection to our Theorem~\ref{labell} that we leave open here. Given a finite union $U$ of \textit{affine} lines in $\mathbb R^d$, under which condition on $U$ does there exist a nonzero harmonic function $F$ such that $F\restr U=0$ ? Note that this question is consistent with the main results of the current paper since a  harmonic function that vanishes on an edge of a cube (which is a segment) should actually vanish on the whole line containing this edge. In effect, this fact holds in a more general setting of real analytic functions (harmonic functions are well known to be real analytic see~\cite{axler2013harmonic}). This means that a real analytic function that vanishes at a relatively open subset of an analytic subvariety, should vanish at the whole subvariety see e.g.~\cite{enciso2013some}. In the paper~\cite{flatto1966level} two particular cases of this question have been considered. Namely the case of linear one dimensional subspaces and that of parallel lines, both in the plane.  

The rest of the current paper is organized as follows. The second section contains our first proof of  Lemma~\ref{lem1}. In the third section, which is the Appendix of this paper, we give an answer to the question mentioned in the last paragraph in the plane in  full generality and give our second proof of Lemma~\ref{lem1}. We will not give proofs of the statements contained in the two remarks just above Lemma~\ref{lem1}, since they are pretty much the same as that of Theorem~\ref{labell}.


