\section{Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem1}}

So, we concentrate on the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem1}.

\begin{proof} 

 Let $V$ denote the Vandermonde polynomial defined by
$$
V(y):=\prod_{1\leqslant i<j\leqslant d}(y_i-y_j).
$$ 
 for all $y\in \mathbb R^d$. Then by~\cite{konig2001eigenvalues}, Lemma 3.1, for any triple of constants $(a,b,c)\in \mathbb R^3$ we have for all $y\in \mathbb R^d$ the following property
$$
\sum_{i=1}^d (y_i^2+ay_i+b)\frac{\partial^2}{\partial y_i^2}V(y)+\sum_{i=1}^d c\frac{\partial}{\partial y_i}V(y)=0,
$$ 
and choosing $a=b=0$ and $c=1/2$ thus yields
$$
2\sum_{i=1}^d y_i^2\frac{\partial^2}{\partial y_i^2}V(y)+\sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial}{\partial y_i}V(y)=0
$$ 
for all $y\in \mathbb R^d$.

On the other hand, we have for all $x\in \mathbb R^d$,
$$
\Delta f_d(x)=\sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial^2}{\partial x_i^2} V(x_1^2,\ldots, x_d^2)= 4\sum_{i=1}^d x_i^2\frac{\partial^2}{\partial x_i^2}V(x)+2\sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}V(x),
$$
which means that $\Delta f_d(x)=0$ and we are done.


