\section{New families of harmonic polynomials vanishing on the same set in the unit ball}

Here is one result that gives examples of linearly independent harmonic functions vanishing at the same set in a ball.


\begin{proop}
Let $\{f_j\}_{j=1}^\infty$ be a linear independent family of functions satisfying $f_j\in \mathcal H(\mathbb R^n)$ with $n\geqslant 3$ for each $j$ and such that there exists $\delta>0$ satisfying $Z_{f_j}\cap B(0,\delta)=Z_{f_i}\cap B(0,\delta)$ for all $(i,j)\in \mathbb N^2$, and let $\varphi: \mathbb R^m \rightarrow \mathbb R^n$ be any non constant harmonic morphism. Then we have
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{one} $\varphi\circ f_j \in \mathcal H(\mathbb R^n)$ for all $j\in \mathbb N$.
\item \label{two}  $\{\varphi\circ f_j\}_j$ is a linearly independent family.
\item \label{three}  There is $\delta_1>0$ such that $Z_{\varphi\circ f_j}\cap B(0,\delta)=Z_{\varphi\circ f_i}\cap B(0,\delta)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proop}
\begin{proof}
The first and the third points above are evident. According to~\cite{ababou1999polynomes}, if $\varphi: \mathbb R^m \rightarrow \mathbb R^n$ is a non constant harmonic morphism then it is surjective. Thus the second point follows as well. 
\end{proof}

Let us now prove Theorem~\ref{labell2}.

\begin{proof}
Let $n$ be a natural number and consider for instance the mapping $\phi=(\phi_1,\phi_2): \mathbb R^{2n}\rightarrow  \mathbb R^2$ defined by 
$$\phi_1(x):=x^2_1-x^2_2+x^2_3-x^2_4+\ldots+x_{2n-1}^2-x_{2n}^2$$ 
and 
$$ \phi_2(x):=2x_1x_2+2x_3x_4+\ldots +2x_{2n-1}x_{2n}.$$ Then, according to~\cite{ou1997quadratic}, Theorem 5.11  this mapping is a harmonic morphism. Thus the irreducible quadratic homogenous harmonic polynomial $$\phi_1(x)=x^2_1-x^2_2+x^2_3-x^2_4+\ldots+x_{2n-1}^2-x_{2n}^2$$  that nontrivially depends on all $2n$ variables of  $\mathbb R^{2n}$  divides infinitely many linearly independent harmonic polynomials, for example $$P_k(x):=\Re(\phi_1(x)+i\phi_2(x))^{2k+1}$$ with natural $k$ will work. Let us formally prove that polynomials $P_k$ form a nontrivial family. Notice that in $\mathbb R^2$ the polynomial $\Re(u+iv)^{2k+1}$ has the following form $$\Re(u+iv)^{2k+1}=u^{2k+1} +\text{terms that contain a multiple of } uv.$$  
This means that the polynomial $P_k$  has unimodular coefficients in front of the monomials  $x_1^{4k+2},\ldots, x_{2n}^{4k+2}$ and is thus nontrivial. Moreover it holds that $\deg(P_k)=4k+2$ so the family is linearly independent.

\bigskip

%In $\mathbb R^5$, we use Th\'eor\`eme 4.3 in~\cite{ababou1999polynomes}. This says that the polynomial 
%$$(\varphi_1(x),\varphi_2(x)):=(\Re(z^2w+2\gamma z t -\gamma^2 \bar{w}), \Im(z^2w+2\gamma z t -\gamma^2 \bar{w})),$$%
%where $x=(z,w,t)\in \mathbb R^5$ with $z=x_1+iy_1, w=x_2+iy_2$ and $t\in \mathbb R$ is a harmonic morphism from $\mathbb R^5$ to $\mathbb C$. Thus it suffices to replace $(\phi_1,\phi_2)$ from the previous considerations by $(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$.

Let us now turn to the case of odd dimensions $m:=2k+1$ with $k\geq 2$. We use the result given by Example 5.2.7 in~\cite{baird2003harmonic}. This says that the polynomial 
$$(\varphi_1(x),\varphi_2(x)):=\xi_1(z)x_1+\ldots+\xi_{m-2}(z)x_{m-2},$$
where $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_m)\in \mathbb R^m$ and $z=x_{m-1}+ix_m$ is a harmonic morphism from $\mathbb R^m$ to $\mathbb C$ once we know that
$$\sum_{j=1}^{m-2}\xi^2_j(z)=0$$
identically. In order to satisfy this last condition, we use ansatz 6.8.6 in~\cite{baird2003harmonic}, which gives
$$\xi:=(1-g^2,i(1+g^2),-2g),$$
where we pose $g^2:=g_1^2 + \ldots + g_{m-2}^2$ and $g:=(g_1,\ldots, g_{m-2})$ for any choice of polynomials $(g_1,\ldots, g_{m-2}).$ Thus it suffices to replace $(\phi_1,\phi_2)$ from the previous considerations by $(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$.

This thus extends results from the fourth section of paper~\cite{logunov2015ratios}.

\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
Notice that this last argument works for all $m\geq 3$. However, for $m=3$ this gives no nontrivial example that we wish to construct, and for $m=4$ we have algready constrcuted one, so we restrict our considerations above to the case $m\geq 5$.
\end{rem}

\begin{rem}
There are many other nontrivial harmonic morphisms between Euclidean spaces. We specifically name those bsaed on Hopf polynomials and refer the interested reader to the book~\cite{baird2003harmonic} for this and other constructions.
\end{rem}

