%Consider an auxiliary sequence of functions in $\mathcal H(\mathbb R^3)$ defined by $\widetilde{f_n}:=f_n/\lambda_n$. We know that the sequence of functions $\widetilde{f_n}$ is uniformly bounded. It hence converges pointwise to a certain function $f\in \mathcal H(\mathbb R^3)$, maybe up to a subsequence $\{x_{n_k}\}_k$. The sequence $\{x_{n_k}\}_k$ has a convergent subsequence. Let $x_0\in \mathbb S$ be its limit. Then we have $\widetilde{f}(x_0)=1$ and $\widetilde{f}\restr{P_1\cup P_2}=0$ EXPLAIN WHY. But the latter property implies that $f\equiv 0$, simply via Schwarz reflection principle for harmonic functions. We hence arrive at the contradiction and the result follows.
