So, we are left with the question to find out what kind of a union $U$ of affine lines can be contained in a nodal set of a nonzero harmonic function in the plane. Thanks to the Schwarz reflection principle for harmonic functions, see e.g.~\cite{axler2013harmonic}, the only case that still needs to be treated is the one where the union $U$ consists of three lines where two of these lines are parallel and the third one intersects them. In the case where this latter line is not orthogonal to the former two lines, easy geometric observations coupled with one more application of the Schwarz reflection principle for harmonic functions, permits us to conclude that any harmonic function that vanishes on this union should actually be equal to zero identically. This means that we can assume from now on that the three lines in question form an `orthogonal'  half-strip. 

Let us now construct a nonzero harmonic function that vanishes at the boundary of the planar half-strip $H:=\{(x_1,x_2)\in \mathbb R^2: 0\leqslant x_2, -\pi/2\leqslant x_1\leqslant\pi/2\}$. We give the answer right away and then comment on how to find it. The function is defined by the following formula
\begin{multline}
\label{halfstrip}
f_0(x_1,x_2):=\frac{\cos(x_1)\sinh(x_2)(\sin^2(x_1)+\sinh^2(x_2))^{1/2}}{\sin(x_1)\cosh(x_2)+(\sin^2(x_1)+\sinh^2(x_2))^{1/2}}\\
\cdot\left(1+\frac{\cos^2(x_1)\sinh^2(x_2)}{(\sin(x_1)\cosh(x_2)+(\sin^2(x_1)+\sinh^2(x_2))^{1/2})^2}\right)^{-1},
\end{multline}
see Figure~\ref{fig} for the graph of the function $f_0$. It is a routine computation to verify that the function $f_0$ is harmonic on the plane and that it is equal to zero at the boundary of the half-strip $H$.

To discover the formula~\eqref{halfstrip}, one can use a conformal mappings from the upper plane onto the strip $S=\{(x_1,x_2)\in \mathbb R^2: -\pi/2\leqslant x_1\leqslant\pi/2\}$ and the half-strip $H$ and the fact that the function $e^{x_1} \sin(x_2)$ is a harmonic function that is zero on the boundary of the entire strip. An alternative function enjoying those properties is given by the following formula $\sinh(x_1)\sin(x_2)$.

