ABSTRACT VECCHIO:
%In this study we explore the ability of three geographical entities (metropolitan areas, regions and countries) to specialise and diversify their innovation portfolio, understanding if the geographical size is relevant to their innovation strategy.
We examine two essential technological indicators: Technological Fitness, which assesses an entity's capacity to diversify and generate sophisticated technologies, and Technological Coherence, which quantifies the degree of specialization based on the similarity of technologies in an entity's technological portfolio.
Based on our findings, the former appears to be important in each of the three geographical scales, highlighting the importance of diversifying highly skilled technologies for economic growth.
For Coherence, instead, the relative importance of the growth of the geographical entities diminishes as the size of the geographical entity grows, and for countries it has a negative impact on growth, indicating that countries need to diversify to increase their GDP per capita.
These findings shed light on the intricate interplay between technological investment and economic development across different geographical scales. Understanding the varying roles of Technological Fitness and Coherence in different contexts can aid policymakers and stakeholders in formulating tailored strategies for technological advancement and development.

