\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{I}{nspired} by natural genetic evolution, Holland introduced the foundational concept of genetic algorithms \cite{Holland1992Adaptation} . Subsequently, leveraging their robust global search capabilities and inherent flexibility, genetic algorithms have found extensive applications across diverse domains including machine learning and multi-objective optimization\cite{Katoch2021Review}.In the ongoing evolution of genetic algorithms, enhancing algorithmic performance through mechanisms of interaction and adaptation has emerged as a significant research thrust. Drawing inspiration from natural phenomena, such as the 'arms race' observed between predators and prey, Competitive Co-evolutionary Algorithms (CCEAs) represent a notable paradigm. Hills pioneered an application where two populations co-evolve; by constructing a co-evolving ‘parasite’ population to challenge the ‘host’ population, this approach partially mitigated the issue of premature convergence to local optima\cite{Hillis1990Coevolving}.Within this co-evolutionary framework, typically two or more competing populations are established, wherein the fitness of an individual in one population is determined by the outcome of its interactions with individuals from the other competing population(s).
Over the subsequent decades, the framework of Competitive Co-evolutionary Algorithms (CCEAs) has been substantially enriched and widely applied. This 'arms race'-style evolutionary process has demonstrated immense potential across numerous domains, achieving remarkable success in areas such as game AI generation\cite{Elfeky_Elsayed_Marsh_Essam_Cochrane_Sims_Sarker_2021,Indaco2023Coevolving,Elfeky2022Coevolutionary,Kelly2024Behaviour,Partlan2022EvolvingBehavior},the biomedical field\cite{Yao2025Coevolutionary,Yang2023Deploying},robotic control\cite{Jones2018Evolving,Kelly2022Environment,Nolfi2025Global},multi-objective optimization\cite{Chen2020Novel,Ma2019Survey,Antonio2018Coevolutionary,Xia2023CoEvolutionary} , and robust design \cite{Zychowski2024Coevolutionary,Meneghini2016Competitive,Li2015Competitive,Li2015New}. Driven by continuous competitive pressure, CCEAs are capable of exploring intricate strategy spaces and evolving highly sophisticated solutions.
However, despite the significant empirical success of co-evolutionary algorithms, their inherent complex evolutionary dynamics, such as intransitivity\cite{Antonio2018Coevolutionary} , the Red Queen effect\cite{Bonachela2017Ecoevolutionary,Cliff1995Tracking,Solé_2022} and disengagement\cite{Cartlidge2004Combating}, often impede stable convergence, potentially leading to performance degradation and strategic cycling. Furthermore, many existing CCEA enhancement methods tend to rely on heuristic mechanisms; while potentially effective for specific problems, they generally lack universal convergence guarantees, particularly within challenging intransitive game environments\cite{Zychowski2018Addressing,Harris2021Competitive}.
Addressing the aforementioned potential issues, we introduce the Marker Gene Method (MGM) and have conducted a rigorous mathematical analysis of its convergence properties. The analytical results demonstrate that MGM can establish strong attractors within the close vicinity of Nash Equilibria (NE) and possesses robust evolutionary dynamics, thereby mitigating premature convergence. The method establishes a dynamic evolutionary benchmark via the marker gene mechanism, directing individuals to primarily evolve along a specific trajectory. Furthermore, a dynamic weight adjust mechanism is incorporated to account for an individual’s performance against other members of the opposing population, thereby addressing issues of frequent directional shifts leading to oscillations and entrapment in local optima. To accelerate convergence and leverage historical information, we have additionally designed an optional memory pool (details are provided in Supplementary Material Section S.II) .This mechanism preserves high-performing marker genes along with their most formidable corresponding opponents, ensuring rapid resolution when encountering analogous challenges. It is important to clarify that the MGM framework and its theoretical analysis, as proposed and validated in this paper, are currently primarily focused on Strictly Competitive Game (SCG) scenarios, wherein the interests of the participants are in direct opposition.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section ~\ref{sec:II}  reviews related work. Section ~\ref{sec:III} details the core mechanisms of our proposed Marker Gene Method (MGM). Section~\ref{sec:IV} validates MGM’s effectiveness through a three-stage experimental approach: first establishing its fundamental convergence in the standard RPS game, then testing its practical value in multi-objective optimization, and finally, by incorporating a Memory Pool (MP), demonstrating its superior ability to solve the highly challenging Shapley Biased Game. Section~\ref{sec:V} concludes the paper and outlines future work.


\section{Related Works and Backgrounds}
\label{sec:II}
\subsection {Intransitivity}
Intransitivity is a prevalent phenomenon within population co-evolution. Jong characterizes intransitivity by a relation $R$ where, if $aRb\land bRc$  are true, the relation $aRc$ does not necessarily hold \cite{Jong_2004}. During coevolution, strategy dominance cycles akin to those in Rock-Paper-Scissors can emerge. This phenomenon can trap the algorithm in cyclical patterns, impeding convergence to a stable state\cite{Yao2025Pathologies}. Conversely, Richter argues that intransitivity can be beneficial by helping maintain population diversity, thereby preventing over-concentration on a single strategy.\cite{Richter2015Coevolutionary}.Completely resolving intransitivity is challenging, as it is often an inherent property of game scenarios involving more than three strategies, particularly in those characterized by high-dimensional strategy spaces\cite{Nolfi1998Coevolving,Szolnoki2014Cyclic}.
From an evolutionary game theory perspective, under conditions of strictly competitive games and the absence of dissipative terms, a coevolutionary game system exhibiting intransitivity behaves as a Hamiltonian system, forming heteroclinic cycles. This prevents the population strategy frequencies from undergoing volume contraction, leading to persistent periodic cycling or oscillations\cite{Szolnoki2014Cyclic}. This cyclical behavior is attributed to the presence of unstable fixed points in the phase space; these fixed points, representing specific population configurations, simultaneously attract and repel various states. Regarding convergence behavior, due to the mutually countervailing intransitive relationships, the population state tends to move along the simplex rather than towards the centroid Nash Equilibrium. For instance, in the Rock-Paper-Scissors model, the dominant population strategies tend to cycle along the $Rock\rightarrow Paper\rightarrow Scissors\rightarrow Rock$ chain, instead of converging to the centroid, i.e., maintaining strategy frequencies at $(1/3,1/3,1/3)$\cite{Yao2025Pathologies}.
In co-evolutionary algorithms, the introduction of finite populations and random mutations causes the system’s dynamics to no longer strictly adhere to those of a Hamiltonian system; nevertheless, cyclical phenomena persist. These cycles no longer manifest as simple periodic loops but are replaced by non-trivial oscillations, exhibiting characteristics of damped or sustained oscillations\cite{Perdigao2023Complexity}. Under the influence of such oscillations, the assessment of individual fitness levels can be significantly affected, potentially leading to strong strategies being misjudged as low-performing during oscillations, thereby constraining the population’s convergence outcomes\cite{Nolfi2025Global}. Ma posited that co-evolutionary systems often exhibit “oscillation phenomena” due to intransitivity, making it difficult for algorithms to find Pareto optimal solutions\cite{Ma2019Survey}.
Fajardo employed the RLS-PD algorithm to achieve rapid search for Nash equilibrium positions; however, it was highly prone to ‘forgetting’ these positions and departing from their close neighborhoods, a forgetting issue that was only mitigated by establishing an archiving mechanism\cite{Fajardo2023Runtime}. Benford, by leveraging bit frequency information, proposed the Univariate Marginal Distribution Algorithm (UMDA), which is capable of finding and maintaining Nash equilibrium positions with high probability\cite{Benford2024Runtime}. However, UMDA relies on observed strategies to estimate distributions, which can become a disadvantage in the absence of necessary diversity maintenance mechanisms. Fajardo later proposed the Ranking Diversity CoEA (RankDivCoEA), which demonstrated excellent performance on specific domain problems; however, its performance under complex intransitive conditions remains an unresolved issue\cite{Fajardo2024Ranking}. Lai, through the concept of priority layering, introduced PL-NSGA-II, which has discovered numerous high-quality solutions within NSGA-II scenarios\cite{Lai2021Solving}. However, this method relies on evident priorities, and in other scenarios where clear priorities may not exist, the approach becomes inapplicable. Żychowski employed the Hall of Fame (HoF) method to store previously encountered superior individuals; while this approach partially addresses the 'reinventing the wheel' problem caused by cyclical effects, practical experiments revealed that the advantages of some individuals can be retained for several future generations (i.e., memory does not dissipate quickly but persists), a phenomenon not considered by HoF, which directly eliminates stored individuals based on fitness ranking\cite{Zychowski2024Coevolutionary}.
Existing methods, when attempting to address the intransitivity problem, often rely on explicit auxiliary information such as archives or distributions, predominantly employ heuristic algorithms, and possess a relatively weak theoretical foundation. In contrast, MGM primarily utilizes its core mechanisms, namely dynamic weights and marker genes, to form strong attractors in the close neighborhood of Nash Equilibria (NE), thereby ensuring convergence to and stability within these regions.

\subsection {Red Queen Effect}

The Red Queen phenomenon, originating from evolutionary biology, describes continuous adaptive competition among species in coevolution: Each species must constantly evolve to maintain its relative fitness, or risk being surpassed by others \cite{Solé_2022}. The driving force behind competitive coevolutionary algorithms stems from Red Queen Dynamics. Red Queen Dynamics, through sustained competitive pressure, can promote population diversity and prevent premature convergence to local optima, and is considered a standard theory for analyzing coevolutionary population dynamics in diverse fields such as economics and ecology \cite{Antero2022Sourcing,Lamsdell2021Conquest,Robson2005Complex}.

However, this dynamic can also lead interacting populations into the dilemma of 'running to stay in the same place', a phenomenon termed the Red Queen effect (s). Cliff posited that the Red Queen Effect arises from inter-species interactions that alter each other’s fitness landscapes\cite{Cliff1995Tracking}. For instance, in predator-prey models, the optimization of one party’s strategy compels the other to respond rapidly, inducing cyclical fitness fluctuations\cite{Sole2014Red}, which can trap the evolutionary process in oscillations, thereby preventing convergence. In host-parasite models, species’ population densities often exhibit alternating dominance, which maintains species diversity, yet the overall fitness may not show significant improvement\cite{Rabajante2015Red}.

From a dynamical systems perspective, the Red Queen Effect can lead to persistent oscillations and chaos attractors. Extensions of the Lotka-Volterra model demonstrate that mutual strategy adjustments between interacting parties result in continuous oscillations of population densities, rather than convergence to stable equilibrium points\cite{Dercole2010Chaotic,Marrow1992Coevolution}. Co-evolution causes the fitness landscape to change dynamically over time, leading populations to continually pursue “currently” optimal strategies, lacking a clear evolutionary direction, and consequently becoming trapped in local optima\cite{Cliff1995Tracking}. Schenk, through comparative evolutionary game models, demonstrated that genetic drift can weaken the persistence of Red Queen Dynamics, but still fails to completely eliminate strategy oscillations\cite{Schenk2020How}.

Żychowski  posited that utilizing multi-objective trade-offs for individual performance could mitigate the dominance of the Red Queen Effect; recognizing that pure competitive co-evolution often becomes trapped in inefficient cycles due to this effect, he proposed guiding the search direction through Postponed Preference Articulation, leading to the development of Memetic CoEvoMOG. However, this algorithm heavily relies on pairwise comparisons based on Theorem 1 to guide evolution. In the presence of intransitivity, such pairwise dominance relationships may fail to establish a stable, transitive order, rendering it difficult for the algorithm to break cycles or identify a clear evolutionary trajectory, consequently limiting its efficacy in complex intransitive scenarios\cite{Zychowski2018Addressing}. Li achieved effective utilization of past memory and dynamic selection of evolutionary strategies by establishing a Heuristic and Historical-Based Evolutionary Operator. However, this strategy selection approach heavily depends on stored memory, potentially leading to misjudgments when memory is absent, and it does not significantly alleviate the problem of intransitivity\cite{Li2024Competitive}. Zhou employed a knowledge-guided approach to provide an evolutionary direction for co-evolution, but this method is heavily reliant on prior knowledge\cite{Zhou2024KnowledgeGuided}.

Given the 'double-edged sword' nature of the Red Queen Effect, balancing exploration and exploitation becomes crucial for mitigating this effect. If the Red Queen Effect is suppressed too strongly, the ability to explore diverse solutions may be lacking; conversely, if the suppression is too weak, severe strategy oscillations can occur, leading to a failure to converge. MGM, through its two core mechanisms—marker genes and dynamic weights—can balance the population’s exploration and exploitation capabilities without requiring additional memory or historical evaluations.


\subsection {Detachment}

Detachment refers to a phenomenon where, if one population (e.g., host or parasite) becomes significantly superior to another, the fitness differences among individuals within the superior population diminish (homogenization), leading to a loss of directional selective pressure, and subsequently, random drift and oscillations in the evolutionary trajectory\cite{Cartlidge2004Combating}. Antonio argued that detachment undermines the co-evolutionary mechanism, transforming co-evolution into a unilaterally dominated “static optimization” process, thereby losing the core advantages of coevolution\cite{Antonio2018Coevolutionary}.

One trigger for detachment is the flattening of the fitness function of the superior population due to homogenization, where vanishing gradients cause natural selection to fail, and genetic drift becomes the primary evolutionary force, leading the population state to a random walk in the strategy space\cite{Dieckmann1996Dynamical}. For instance, in predator-prey models, if the prey population detaches from coevolution due to a fitness advantage, its fitness landscape may become static, losing the co-evolutionarily driven capability for diversity exploration\cite{Rikvold2009Complex,Dercole2006Coevolution}. Another cause stems from differing evolutionary rates between the co-evolving parties; decoupling can then lead to an “evolutionary lag,” resulting in the complete separation of their evolutionary trajectories\cite{Dercole2006Coevolution}. Marshal, in long-term experimental evolution, observed that E. coli’s cell size and metabolic cost evolved independently after decoupling, forming uncorrelated evolutionary paths\cite{Marshall2022Longterm}.

With the disappearance of natural selection and co-evolutionary interactions, traditional co-evolutionary algorithms risk degenerating into single-objective optimization methods, thereby forfeiting their multi-objective trade-off capabilities\cite{Ma2019Survey}. When the co-evolutionary relationship is not entirely severed, oscillatory outcomes resulting from local competition can still emerge; for instance, Kim observed in a multi-layer network co-evolution model that nodes, despite evolving independently in different layers post-decoupling, continued to exhibit periodic oscillations driven by residual interactions\cite{Kim2013Coevolution}.

From a dynamical perspective, both Red Queen Effects and detachment exhibit strong correlations with evolutionary rates and fitness landscapes, and their manifestations in convergence outcomes are also similar. A combined analysis of detachment and Red Queen Effects reveals that for competitive co-evolution, the stability of the evolutionary direction and the rational evaluation of fitness directly influence the actual convergence outcomes. Consequently, the methodological approaches for mitigating both detachment and Red Queen Effects share considerable similarities. Żychowski employed an alternating evolution approach to stabilize the evolutionary process, but this still relied on a fixed number of generations for switching evolutionary phases; an improper selection of this switching interval can lead to the population experiencing either overfitting or underfitting\cite{Zychowski2024Coevolutionary}.

The susceptibility of CCEAs to dynamics such as Red Queen Effects, detachment, and cyclical phenomena arising from intransitivity underscores the need for more sophisticated mechanisms capable of stabilizing the evolutionary process, providing more robust evaluation signals, and effectively leveraging information from the co-evolutionary history. This has spurred the exploration of novel frameworks, such as the Marker Gene Method (MGM) proposed in this paper. MGM aims to directly address these limitations by introducing explicit mechanisms for stability and adaptability, thereby better navigating coevolutionary cycles.



\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:III}
\subsection {Problem Formulation}
Establishing convergence proofs for co-evolutionary algorithms under general game settings is a notoriously challenging problem. To ensure analytical rigor, our current convergence proof and analysis of MGM are therefore specifically conducted for the class of Strictly Competitive Games (SCGs).

Alder defines a Strictly Competitive Game (SCG) as a game $((A,-B))$ where, for any two mixed strategy pairs   $((x, y))$ and  $\ ((x^\prime,\ y^\prime))$, the expressions $x^TAy-{x^\prime}^TAy^\prime$ and $\ x^TBy-{x^\prime}^TBy^\prime$ share the same sign \cite{Adler2009Note}. Effectively, an SCG is a two-player game wherein if both players alter their mixed strategies, either the expected payoffs remain constant, or one player’s expected payoff increases while the other’s decreases. Consequently, all mixed strategy pairs in such a game are Pareto optimal. The formal definition of an SCG is presented as follows:
\begin{definition} \label{def:two_player_game} % 使用我们定义好的环境，并给它一个标签以便引用
    A two-player game $G$ is defined as a tuple $ G=(S_1,S_2,u_1,u_2)$, where $S_1$ and $S_2$ are the strategy sets for player 1 and player 2, respectively, and $ u_1:S_1\times S_2\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ and $u_2:S_1\times S_2\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ are their respective payoff functions.
\end{definition}


\begin{definition} \label{def:scg} % 再次使用同一个环境，LaTeX会自动编号为 "Definition 2"
    A game $G=(S_1,S_2,u_1,u_2)$ is a Strictly Competitive Game (SCG) if and only if for any two strategy profiles $(s_1,s_2)$ and $(s_1^\prime,s_2^\prime)\in S_1\times S_2$, the following condition holds:
    \begin{equation} % 使用 equation 环境来展示重要的公式
        u_1(s_1,s_2) > u_1(s_1^\prime,s_2^\prime) \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad u_2(s_1,s_2) < u_2(s_1^\prime,s_2^\prime)
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}

For instance, in a co-evolutionary SCG framework, the success (payoff) of one population directly corresponds to the failure of the other. This represents scenarios of pure conflict or direct competition, such as predator-prey relationships, where the predator’s successful capture is equivalent to the prey’s failure to escape.



\subsection{The Marker Gene Method Overview}

To counteract the instability inherent in Competitive Co-evolutionary Algorithms (CCEAs), such as intransitivity and the Red Queen effect, we propose the Marker Gene Method (MGM). MGM provides a stable evolutionary gradient by evaluating individuals against two distinct criteria. Each population maintains a long-term \textbf{Marker Gene} that serves as a stable benchmark for solution quality. The fitness of an individual is then calculated as a weighted sum of: \textbf{1) Base Fitness}, its performance against this stable marker gene, and \textbf{2) Generalize Fitness}, its average performance against the current opponent population. This dual-fitness evaluation steers evolution towards robust solutions, avoiding the pitfalls of relying solely on transient, snapshot-based fitness.

MGM is operationalized through two primary mechanisms:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A Dynamic Weight Adjustment Mechanism} to balance the influence of Base and Generalize fitness.
\item \textbf{A Marker Gene Update Mechanism} to ensure the benchmark’s stability and relevance.
\end{enumerate}
While this paper focuses on validating this core framework (assuming fitness maximization), we note that an optional Memory Pool (MP) component can further accelerate convergence on pathological problems, as shown in our experiments on the Shapley Biased Game. For clarity and to demonstrate the power of the core mechanisms, our main experiments on CR-MOEA/D and RPS do not include the MP module.

