\section{Empirical Evaluation }
\label{sec:IV}
\subsection{Validation of Theoretical Analysis on SCG}

\subsubsection{Motivation and Experimental Goal}

To validate the convergence properties of the MGM algorithm, we design evolutionary game experiments based on the classic Rock-Paper-Scissors (RPS) model. RPS is a canonical Strictly Competitive Game (SCG) with a well-defined and easily understood Nash equilibrium. Moreover, RPS exhibits pronounced non-transitivity, which in standard competitive coevolutionary algorithms gives rise to cyclic strategies. This makes it an ideal benchmark to evaluate MGM’s ability to enhance stability and convergence. The main objective of this experiment is to empirically test whether MGM can effectively mitigate these cycles and steer the evolving population toward the theoretical NE, thereby providing concrete experimental evidence in support of our convergence analysis.To further investigate the impact of the memory pool (MP) component on MGM’s convergence, we conducted an ablation study by enabling the MP mechanism to assess its necessity in the RPS experiments,detail information can be found in Supplementary Material.

\subsubsection{Experiment Setting}

We adopt the standard RPS game with payoff matrix:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -1 & 1 \\
1 & 0 & -1 \\
-1 & 1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\]


The unique Nash equilibrium is the mixed strategy where each pure action is played with probability \( p = \frac{1}{3} \), corresponding in coevolution to a population state in which the three strategies coexist equally.

We use a two-population coevolution model, each population comprising 100 individuals ($Population Size = 100$ ). Each individual's strategy is a 3-dimensional probability vector for rock, paper, and scissors, constrained to be nonnegative and sum to 1. Crossover rate is 1; mutation rate is 0.1, implemented as Gaussian perturbation with strength 0.05.

Since the RPS Game is a zero-sum game,we set the target fitness \( l = -0.005 \) and base weight $\omega = 0.9$ , and eliminate the worst 20\% of the individuals at each update. The computed thresholds are $KeepCountThreshold = 5$  and $BufferCountThreshold = 5$ . 

\subsubsection{Results}

We compare MGM against three baselines in the standard RPS game, a prototypical intransitive environment. Baseline A omits all MGM mechanisms; Baseline B adds a Hall of Fame; Baseline C uses a Pareto‐update rule.Fig.~\ref{comaprison_base} quantitatively tracks convergence by measuring the KL divergence to the Nash Equilibrium (NE). This comparison decisively shows MGM’s superiority, as its KL divergence steadily decreases while the baselines stagnate at a much higher level.Further information on the complete experimental runs is presented in the supplementary materials.

Analysis of the baseline dynamics reveals classic CCEA failure modes: Baselines A and B become trapped in persistent intransitive cycles, while Baseline C stagnates due to a loss of diversity. (Detailed visualizations confirming these behaviors are available in the Supplementary Material.) In contrast, MGM (Fig.~\ref{mgm_rps_standard}) exhibits markedly stable behavior, quickly converging to a tight NE neighborhood. Critically, its marker gene (lower panel) continues to update frequently even after the population stabilizes, demonstrating an ongoing search that prevents stagnation and ensures stable convergence. These results clearly show that MGM’s mechanisms effectively guide the population to the NE, a conclusion strongly supported by the superior KL divergence shown in Fig.~\ref{comaprison_base}.

