\subsubsection{Discussion and Link to Theory}

The RPS results strongly validate MGM’s core mechanisms and its theoretical advantages in addressing challenges of competitive coevolution, notably its ability to stabilize evolutionary direction, sustain momentum, and guide the population toward a stable solution.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Mechanism for NE stability}
        \begin{itemize} % 使用itemize或直接段落描述也可以，这里用itemize强调子步骤
            \item \textbf{Marker Gene (MG):} As the primary driving force, MG imposes continuous, adaptive strong selection pressure. Near the NE, MG switches at low frequency before stabilization and at high frequency afterward (Fig.~\ref{mgm_rps_standard} (c) and (d)), providing a clear evolutionary focal point that anchors the population around the NE.
            \item \textbf{Dynamic Weights (DW):} These support and amplify MG’s guidance by adaptively balancing exploitation of the current strongest MG and exploration of the broader strategy space, promoting smooth convergence to a robust mixed‐strategy NE, preventing overfitting, and ensuring a strong basin of attraction around the NE.
        \end{itemize}

  \item \textbf{Driving robustness via strong adaptive pressure:}
           The persistent high‐frequency, low‐amplitude fluctuations of MG during the stable phase are not signs of instability but an intrinsic, constructive mechanism—an ongoing 'adversarial test' or 'stress test' that compels the population to select strategies capable of countering the strongest threat represented by MG. Ultimately, only truly robust strategies that generalize across multiple strong opponents prevail and stabilize. This internal perturbation is MGM’s active means of achieving robustness.
\end{enumerate}

The RPS experiments clearly demonstrate how MGM’s unique combination of marker genes, dynamic weights, and memory pool overcomes core SCG challenges, not only achieving NE stability but proactively driving evolution toward robust solutions, in line with its theoretical design. 

\subsubsection{Population Size Effects and Validation of Convergence Properties}

To empirically test Theorems C.15, D.6, E.3, F.5, and G.5 from the Supplementary Materials—especially regarding the exponential decay of extreme-event probabilities with the number of evaluations and its impact on system stability—we compare the convergence of MGM under different population sizes. Specifically, we reduce the population from $N=100$ to $N=50$, and repeat the RPS experiment 10 times. 

The results are quantitatively summarized in Fig.~\ref{mgm_rps_popsize_effects}. As predicted by our theoretical analysis, the larger population ($N=100$) demonstrates significantly superior convergence. Its mean KL divergence to the Nash Equilibrium (NE) is consistently lower and more stable, as indicated by the narrower confidence intervals. Conversely, the smaller population ($N=50$) exhibits a higher and more volatile KL divergence, signifying a weaker convergence to a less stable equilibrium. This directly supports our theorems: a larger population size provides more evaluations, which exponentially reduces the probability of disruptive ranking-reversal events and allows the system to settle into the NE's strong basin of attraction. Comprehensive details regarding the individual experimental runs are available in the supplementary materials.
