We evaluated MGM and C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D using common metrics in robust multi-objective optimization: Hypervolume (HV), Spacing, and perturbation severity. Since both MGM and C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D explore strong perturbations, the Inverted Generational Distance (IGD) metric is excluded due to its limited ability to objectively assess solution sets under perturbation; instead, perturbation severity is used. When computing perturbation severity, we use the same weight distribution for cross‐validation and measure two effects in parallel: (1) the performance degradation of MGM under perturbations generated by C‐RMOEA/D, and (2) the performance degradation of C‐RMOEA/D under perturbations generated by MGM. For each weight setting, we take the average degradation caused by an algorithm and call it its Severity. We also introduce a metric called Performance Degradation, which evaluates an algorithm’s robustness by averaging its maximum observed performance drop.

\subsubsection{Results}

The result on Fig.~\ref{zdt2-pareto} visually compare the Pareto fronts obtained by C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D and MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D. It is clear from the plots that the Pareto front found by MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D largely dominates that of C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D. Fig.~\ref{perfermance}(a) shows that  MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D achieves a significantly higher HV than baseline C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D, while also exhibiting a lower Spacing value(Fig.~\ref{perfermance}(b)). A higher HV indicates that the front obtained by MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D is superior in both convergence and diversity. A lower Spacing implies a more uniform distribution of solutions along the front for MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D. Overall, these findings demonstrate that integrating MGM enables C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D to locate higher-quality Pareto sets under worst-case perturbations.

By cross-validation on Fig.~\ref{perfermance}(c) for average perturbation severity, MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D outperforms C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D by approximately 37.8\%. This result indicates that MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D can explore more severe perturbations, consistent with our RPS experiments’ premise of proactive exploration of strong perturbations.However, Fig.~\ref{perfermance}(d) indicates that in terms of average worst-case perturbation resistance, MGM-R-MOEA\textbackslash{}D is slightly inferior to C-RMOEA\textbackslash{}D by about 5.27\%.We infer that by actively seeking out more challenging ‘opponents’ (perturbations), MGM drives the population towards a Pareto front that is globally superior, yet potentially more vulnerable at specific points. This can be viewed as a ‘high-risk, high-reward’ strategy, where the primary objective is to enhance overall dominance—as evidenced by the Hypervolume (HV) indicator—rather than to ensure absolute invincibility at every individual point .

