With the recognition that logic at first would seem to serve as the `archetype of order', the writings in the so-called later phase redirected the attention to ordinary language, although this did not mean abandoning the view of language as a calculus, a (language-)game in which learning and understanding would be like being capable of seeing the role, the use and the purpose of words, signs, sentences in the game of language. With his own words, the intention was `to know what makes language language':
