\begin{abstract} 
To address the limitations of traditional reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RIS) in spatial control capability, this paper introduces the concept of the fluid antenna system (FAS) and proposes a fluid simultaneously transmitting and reflecting RIS (FSTAR-RIS) assisted non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) multi-user communication system. In this system, each FSTAR-RIS element is capable of flexible mobility and can dynamically adjust its position in response to environmental variations, thereby enabling simultaneous service to users in both the transmission and reflection zones. This significantly enhances the system’s spatial degrees of freedom (DoF) and service adaptability.
To maximize the system’s weighted sum-rate, we formulate a non-convex optimization problem that jointly optimizes the base station beamforming, the transmission/reflection coefficients of the FSTAR-RIS, and the element positions. An alternating optimization (AO) algorithm is developed, incorporating successive convex approximation (SCA), semi-definite relaxation (SDR), and majorization-minimization (MM) techniques. In particular, to address the complex channel coupling introduced by the coexistence of direct and FSTAR-RIS paths, the MM framework is employed in the element position optimization subproblem, enabling an efficient iterative solution strategy.
Simulation results validate that the proposed system achieves up to a 27\% increase in total sum rate compared to traditional STAR-RIS systems and requires approximately 50\% fewer RIS elements to attain the same performance, highlighting its effectiveness for cost-efficient large-scale deployment.
\end{abstract}


\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Fluid simultaneously transmitting and reflecting reconfigurable intelligent surfaces, non-orthogonal multiple access, alternating optimization algorithm
\end{IEEEkeywords}



\IEEEpeerreviewmaketitle



\section{Introduction}
With the emergence of beyond 5G (B5G) and 6G systems, the growing demand for massive connectivity and limited spectrum resources necessitates high spectral efficiency and enhanced user access \cite{9509294}. Non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA), by enabling power-domain multiplexing, improves spectrum utilization and mitigates access congestion \cite{9693417}. Its integration with advanced wireless technologies has shown great promise in dense multi-user scenarios \cite{10504837}. Meanwhile, reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) has gained attention for its ability to reconfigure wireless channels via low-cost passive elements, enhancing coverage, energy efficiency, and interference control \cite{10596064}.


%Although conventional RIS has demonstrated notable advantages in enhancing communication performance, its inherent structural limitation confines signal manipulation to reflection only, thereby restricting its ability to simultaneously serve users on both sides \cite{10133841}. To address this limitation, simultaneously transmitting and reflecting reconfigurable intelligent surface (STAR-RIS) has been proposed, where each element is capable of simultaneously or selectively transmitting and reflecting electromagnetic waves \cite{9570143}. This structural advancement enables full-space coverage, significantly broadening the application scope of RIS, particularly in scenarios with spatially distributed users. Building on this foundation, the integration of STAR-RIS and NOMA has emerged as a promising approach to further enhance spectral efficiency and support massive user connectivity. Recent studies have demonstrated the effectiveness of this integration in diverse multi-user communication environments. In the study of \cite{9863732}, a STAR-RIS-assisted NOMA system was proposed, where a two-layer iterative algorithm was developed to jointly optimize decoding order, power allocation, active beamforming, and reflection coefficients. The work in \cite{10472878} investigated a STAR-RIS-assisted integrated sensing and communications (ISAC) system by dividing the coverage into sensing and communication regions, and enhanced communication security via joint optimization of beamforming and artificial jamming. In \cite{10820060}, a STAR-RIS-assisted full-duplex system was studied, where STAR-RIS was deployed at the cell edge, and analytical expressions for uplink and downlink rates were derived under hardware impairments and residual interference. The study in \cite{10380743} focused on covert communication in STAR-RIS-assisted NOMA systems with imperfect SIC, and optimized the effective covert rate by analyzing detection error probability and adjusting the transmission-reflection ratio. In \cite{10440372}, the authors explored STAR-RIS-assisted NOMA for ultra-reliable low-latency communication (URLLC) scenarios under imperfect CSI, and proposed a robust alternating optimization framework to jointly design beamforming at both the BS and STAR-RIS, achieving noticeable spectral efficiency gains while satisfying URLLC constraints. Moreover, the work in \cite{11010152} proposed a STARS-UAV-NOMA system for outdoor-to-indoor coverage, where joint optimization of UAV trajectory, user pairing, and STAR-RIS beamforming was conducted to maximize the average weighted sum-rate via a penalty-based algorithm.

Although conventional RIS enhances communication performance, its reflection-only capability limits full-space coverage and user support. To overcome this, simultaneously transmitting and reflecting reconfigurable intelligent surface (STAR-RIS) has been introduced, enabling each element to simultaneously or selectively transmit and reflect signals \cite{9570143}, thus extending RIS functionality to both sides and enhancing deployment flexibility \cite{10133841}. On this basis, integrating STAR-RIS with NOMA has gained attention due to its potential for improved spectral efficiency. Recent studies have explored STAR-RIS-NOMA in diverse scenarios, including multi-user communication \cite{9863732}, covert transmission \cite{10380743}, URLLC \cite{10440372}, ISAC \cite{10472878}, and full-duplex systems \cite{10820060},  demonstrating its adaptability to complex communication demands.

Despite the significant potential of RIS, including STAR-RIS, in reshaping the wireless propagation environment, their structural rigidity remains a fundamental limitation. Most existing RIS designs adopt fixed and uniformly spaced element layouts, which restrict the spatial degrees of freedom (DoF) of the system, leading to performance bottlenecks in beamforming accuracy, adaptability, and interference suppression \cite{11023100}. To enhance system performance, a common approach is to increase the number of RIS elements to achieve higher array gains. However, as the number of elements grows, the associated channel estimation and feedback overhead increase significantly \cite{9409636}. Moreover, an excessive number of elements introduces considerable circuit power consumption \cite{10480438}. Consequently, how to realize higher spatial control capabilities with fewer RIS elements has become an appealing and meaningful research direction.

In recent years, the fluid antenna system (FAS) has attracted growing attention as a flexible architecture that enables dynamic repositioning of antenna elements within a predefined region \cite{10753482}. This added spatial DoF improves channel quality, interference suppression, and user coverage \cite{10599127}, with notable performance gains demonstrated in various scenarios  \cite{10539238}, \cite{10949741}.
Given that FAS has shown significant potential in enhancing the spatial regulation ability of communication systems, some researchers have begun to attempt to introduce its ideas into RIS and have proposed the concept of fluid-RIS. This structure allows each unit to move flexibly within the predefined area, with position regulation as a new degree of freedom, and collaboratively optimizes with the traditional phase control mechanism, thereby further enhancing the system performance.

In the existing work, the authors of \cite{10430366} proposed a mobile RIS architecture based on the concept of fluid antennas and designed a unified non-uniform discrete phase shift strategy, which effectively improves the system performance under Rician fading channels. The authors of \cite{ye2025joint} developed an ISAC system assisted by fluid-RIS. By jointly optimizing the symbol detector, transmit beamformer, and the phase shifts and positions of the fluid-RIS elements, their design significantly reduces sensing mismatch and communication errors while enhancing the performance of multi-target sensing and multi-user communication. The work in \cite{salem2025first} focuses on both single-user single-input single-output (SISO) and multi-user multiple-input single-output (MISO) scenarios, where the positions and phase shifts of the fluid-RIS elements are jointly optimized to achieve a notable improvement in system throughput.

Although the concept of fluid-RIS has shown promising potential, existing studies remain scarce and exhibit several critical limitations. First, most of the current works are based on orthogonal multiple access (OMA), which inherently limits spectral efficiency compared to non-orthogonal schemes. Second, prior research has primarily focused on integrating fluid architectures with conventional RIS designs, while no existing work has systematically investigated the integration of fluid structures with STAR-RIS architectures, restricting their applicability to more complex and full-space coverage scenarios.

Inspired by this, this paper proposes a fluid STAR-RIS (FSTAR-RIS) integrated with NOMA to assist multi-user communication systems. The specific contributions are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A novel multi-user communication architecture is proposed, where each FSTAR-RIS element supports both transmission and reflection functionalities and can flexibly move within a predefined area. This enables simultaneous service to users in both transmission and reflection zones, thereby enhancing the spatial degrees of freedom. 

    \item The integration of NOMA further enhances spectral efficiency and user connectivity, but also introduces highly non-convex constraints, significantly increasing the complexity of the joint optimization problem.

    \item We formulate a challenging non-convex joint optimization problem that simultaneously involves the beamforming design at the base station (BS), the transmission/reflection coefficients of the FSTAR-RIS, and the positions of its elements. To tackle the strong variable coupling, we propose a structured three-layer AO framework, where each subproblem is addressed using appropriate techniques such as semi-definite relaxation (SDR), successive convex approximation (SCA), and majorization-minimization (MM), thus reducing the complexity of the original problem.

    \item To overcome the increased coupling among all involved channels caused by the coexistence of direct and FSTAR-RIS-assisted transmission/reflection paths, we propose an MM-based position optimization method. This approach constructs a tractable surrogate function and iteratively refines the element positions, enabling efficient resolution of the otherwise intractable non-convex subproblem.

    \item Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed FSTAR-RIS-NOMA system outperforms existing benchmark schemes in multi-user communication scenarios. Specifically, with the same number of RIS elements, it achieves up to a {27\% increase in total sum rate} compared to traditional STAR-RIS systems. Moreover, to achieve the same total sum rate, the proposed system requires {approximately 50\% fewer RIS elements}, highlighting its potential to reduce deployment cost and system complexity in large-scale STAR-RIS applications.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is structured as detailed below. The proposed model of the FSTAR-RIS-NOMA assisted communication system is outlined in Section
II, followed by the
problem formulation of the total sum rate maximization.
Section III illustrates the particulars of the proposed alternating algorithms for optimizing the beamforming of the BS, the reflecting and transmitting coefficients of the FSTAR-RIS, and the positions of the FSTAR-RIS elements, respectively. 
Section IV showcases numerical outcomes to assess the efficacy of the proposed algorithm compared with a benchmark scheme. Our conclusion is presented in Section V.

