\subsection{Duffing Oscillator}
A Duffing oscillator is a representative nonlinear dynamical system describing the motion of a damped oscillator with cubic stiffness nonlinearity. The governing equation is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:duffing}
m\ddot{x}(t) + c\dot{x}(t) + kx(t) + k_3x^3(t) = u(t),
\end{equation}
where $m = 1.0\, \mathrm{kg}$, $c = 0.1\, \mathrm{Ns/m}$, $k = 1.5\, \mathrm{N/m}$, and $k_3 = 10\, \mathrm{N/m}^3$, resulting in strongly nonlinear dynamics. The system is excited by a sinusoidal input $u(t) = \sin(\pi t)$. Data are simulated at a sampling frequency of 10 Hz over 60 seconds, with the first 20 seconds used for training and the remaining 40 seconds for testing.

