To quantitatively assess model performance, we compute mean squared errors (MSE) for both predicted accelerations and inferred latent states compared to ground truth. The mean and standard deviation of MSE values across three independent runs are reported in Table~\ref{tab:duffing}. All models achieve similar performance in predicting accelerations, though SKANODE consistently shows slightly superior accuracy. However, significant differences arise in the recovery of latent states: SKANODE achieves the lowest MSE and variance, demonstrating its ability to extract physically interpretable dynamics reliably across repeated trials. In contrast, ANODE exhibits high variance and poor latent state interpretability, verifying that ANODE tends to learn arbitrary latent representations to reconstruct observables. SONODE yields more stable but still non-interpretable latent trajectories, as its structure lacks explicit alignment with the true physical coordinates. Furthermore, we also evaluate a structured state-space Neural ODE using a conventional multilayer perceptron (MLP) in place of KAN---referred to as S$^3$NODE in Table~\ref{tab:duffing}---implemented as a 5-layer MLP with 32 hidden units per layer. The results demonstrate that the superior performance of SKANODE is not only due to its structural inductive biases in the latent and observation models, but also to the symbolic discovery capability of KAN, which enhances both interpretability and predictive accuracy, by uncovering explicit governing equations and improving the estimation of system quantities.

Importantly, SKANODE not only improves interpretability but also contributes to better predictive performance, highlighting the value of incorporating physics-informed structure and symbolic discovery. To further examine robustness, Table~\ref{tab:duffing} presents the results under varying levels of observation noise. Notably, ANODE encounters severe convergence issues under noisy conditions, while SKANODE maintains stable performance across all scenarios.

